@@ -141,13 +141,13 @@ Field rules:
 - characterGender: choose the male or female visual avatar that best fits the brief. This selects an appearance, not a restriction on the character identity.
 - characterAge: use 35 (adult). The currently available male and female avatar sets are adults.
 - characterEthnicity: exactly one of "african", "east-asian", "european", "indigenous", "mediterranean", "middle-eastern", "north-african", "pacific-islander", "south-asian", "southeast-asian". Choose the closest facial appearance. Do not return "mixed".
-- characterClothesId and characterHairId: choose suitable defaults from the supplied published wardrobe for the selected gender. Each clothes line is "id: name (role; outfit; formality)", with labels only where they are known; choose clothes that fit characterRole and the setting. Copy exact ids only (the part before the colon); use an empty string for the standard white T-shirt or default brown hair when no better option is appropriate. Do not invent ids. Appearance choices are editable suggestions for the author to confirm.
-- suggestedClothesIds: up to three other clothes ids from the same gender's list that would also suit the character, best first. Do not repeat characterClothesId. Return an empty array when nothing else fits.
+- characterClothesId and characterHairId: choose suitable defaults from the supplied published wardrobe for the selected gender. Clothes are an object: each key is an outfit id and its value describes the outfit as "name (role; outfit; formality)", with labels only where they are known (some values are empty); choose clothes that fit characterRole and the setting. Return a key exactly as written, never its description; use an empty string for the standard white T-shirt or default brown hair when no better option is appropriate. Do not invent ids. Appearance choices are editable suggestions for the author to confirm.
+- suggestedClothesIds: up to three other keys from the same gender's clothes object that would also suit the character, best first. Do not repeat characterClothesId. Return an empty array when nothing else fits.
 - characterPersonality: instructions the model reads while playing this character. Write in second person and start with "You are". Describe stable tone, traits, mannerisms, and speaking style that should follow this character between experiences. Example: "You are cheerful, attentive, and gently humorous. You speak with a soft, melodic tone and often use polite honorifics. You listen closely and make people feel welcome." Do not write a third-person biography, and do not use the character's name or she/he/they as the subject. Do not include the current scenario, participant task, set details, or situation-specific objectives.
 - characterInstructions: instructions the model reads while playing this character. Write in second person ("you", "your"). Cover reusable identity, background, expertise, persistent relationships, rules, and boundaries that should follow this character between experiences. Example: "You have worked at your family's restaurant for years and know the menu by heart. You are proud of your heritage and the food you serve. Stay professional yet warm. Share a short anecdote about the restaurant or the neighborhood only when asked. Avoid over-familiarity, and treat regular customers like old friends." Do not narrate in the third person. Do not include facts, goals, events, or constraints that apply only to this experience or set.
 - setName: a concise, reusable name for the visual setting where the conversation happens. Name the place, not the activity.
 - setDescription: a reusable visual description of that setting for backdrop generation. Describe what the place looks like, the time of day, and the weather. Do not include the participant's task, the plot, or character instructions. At most 800 characters.
-- suggestedSetRefs: up to three numbers from the supplied publicSets list (each line is "number: set name (collection)") for the listed sets that best fit setName, setDescription, and the brief, best first. Prefer the same kind of place and the same world or period (modern, historical, fantasy, science fiction). Copy numbers exactly; return an empty array when no listed set fits well. Do not invent numbers.
+- suggestedSetRefs: up to three numbers from the supplied publicSets list (each line is "number: set name (collection)") for the listed sets that best fit setName, setDescription, and the brief, best first. Suggest only sets from the brief's own world or period (modern, historical, fantasy, science fiction) that show the same kind of place as the brief; when the list has no such set, return an empty array. Copy numbers exactly. Do not invent numbers.
 - startingMessage: the exact first words spoken by the character. This is especially important. Write a natural opening in the character's voice that immediately establishes the situation and gives the participant something meaningful to respond to. Prefer one or two short sentences. Do not use stage directions, narration, quotation marks, labels, placeholders, or a generic "How can I help?" unless that truly fits the scenario. Do not speak for the participant or reveal information meant to remain hidden.
 - systemInstructions: additional situation-specific instructions the model reads while playing this character. This is especially important. Write in second person ("you", "your") and refer to the human as "the participant". In clear present-tense prose, establish the immediate context, the participant's role, your objective, relevant facts, constraints, desired progression, and how you should respond to likely choices. Preserve participant agency and any deliberate mystery. Include concrete scenario facts needed for a consistent conversation, but do not repeat generic character identity or personality. Do not write in the third person, script the participant, mention prompts or models, or include implementation commentary. Write a detailed runnable brief with compact sections, normally 2,800–4,500 characters. Use the author brief as fixed direction and flesh out unspecified interaction design creatively, without fabricating real-world facts or unsupported capabilities.
 
